(6490629, 964130, 43463, '<td valign="top">Garlic butter drenched on prawns - looks mouthwatering. Paired with the coconut rice it probably tastes delightful! Great presentation of the dish it looks very appealing and inviting. The main delicious look of the main dish makes me wonder what other good tasting food is in store with the bowl and ''cup'' I see in the backdrop (bowl looks like it might have a colorful appetizer soup...)Bottom line you made and presented an inviting dish that welcomes the viewer to take a seat and dig in. High marks for a job very well done.</td>', 'Garlic butter drenched on prawns - looks mouthwatering. Paired with the coconut rice it probably tastes delightful! Great presentation of the dish it looks very appealing and inviting. The main delicious look of the main dish makes me wonder what other good tasting food is in store with the bowl and ''cup'' I see in the backdrop (bowl looks like it might have a colorful appetizer soup...)Bottom line you made and presented an inviting dish that welcomes the viewer to take a seat and dig in. High marks for a job very well done.', '2011-08-03 09:53:35.000000', NULL, 1);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(6490649, 963633, 43463, '<td valign="top">Love the way the light plays on the surface of the banana bread - it does indeed look moist. The golden brown is not only warm and inviting but it calls up the sense that it is rich, sweet and buttery in taste. Just a small critique - I wish the crop was not so tight - just pull back a tad so that the edge of the pan holding the middle banana bread is visible and some more of the back end of the loaf is visible. The dessert bread will still be large and dominate the scene, but it give us more of this main dessert (in sharp focus) is available for tasting.</td>', 'Love the way the light plays on the surface of the banana bread - it does indeed look moist. The golden brown is not only warm and inviting but it calls up the sense that it is rich, sweet and buttery in taste. Just a small critique - I wish the crop was not so tight - just pull back a tad so that the edge of the pan holding the middle banana bread is visible and some more of the back end of the loaf is visible. The dessert bread will still be large and dominate the scene, but it give us more of this main dessert (in sharp focus) is available for tasting.', '2011-08-03 10:02:38.000000', NULL, 1),
(6490679, 964258, 43463, '<td valign="top">Love the show of ingredients for the recipe and the <i>arrangement</i> of elements is appealing, but there are a few things that could further boost the appeal of the shot. First I think the horizontal does not complement the image. A vertical presentation would be better because it would complement and flow with the shape of the milk bottle. Plus, a crop on the vertical would allow you to bring your viewers in for a closer look at the details and textures present in your ingredients. There is to much ''empty'' space in the horizontal crop that does very little for your composition. A closer look would show off the textures of the coffee beans, the grains of sugar in the bowl, the textured surface of the eggs...Lighting also appears a bit flat - the milk and the sugar appear more biege than the typical pure white we normally think. Playing with the WB (white balance) on the camera could help adjust it to a more pure white (the backdrop also has a pinkish tone). Natural lighting (some tungsten bulbs give off a yellowish cast so choose your bulbs carefully in artificial lighting)  could also help with maybe some small spotlights behind the bowl of sugar and the bottle of milk. The spotlights behind those two elements will ''outline'' or deepen the the contours of the shapes of these items making them pop from the backdrop.</td>', 'Love the show of ingredients for the recipe and the arrangement of elements is appealing, but there are a few things that could further boost the appeal of the shot. First I think the horizontal does not complement the image. A vertical presentation would be better because it would complement and flow with the shape of the milk bottle. Plus, a crop on the vertical would allow you to bring your viewers in for a closer look at the details and textures present in your ingredients. There is to much ''empty'' space in the horizontal crop that does very little for your composition. A closer look would show off the textures of the coffee beans, the grains of sugar in the bowl, the textured surface of the eggs...Lighting also appears a bit flat - the milk and the sugar appear more biege than the typical pure white we normally think. Playing with the WB (white balance) on the camera could help adjust it to a more pure white (the backdrop also has a pinkish tone). Natural lighting (some tungsten bulbs give off a yellowish cast so choose your bulbs carefully in artificial lighting)  could also help with maybe some small spotlights behind the bowl of sugar and the bottle of milk. The spotlights behind those two elements will ''outline'' or deepen the the contours of the shapes of these items making them pop from the backdrop.', '2011-08-03 10:14:58.000000', NULL, 1),
