(6492174, 963629, 58610, '<td valign="top">I think a deeper dof, which would have kept everything in focus, would have served this shot better. It''s a great concept and setup.</td>', 'I think a deeper dof, which would have kept everything in focus, would have served this shot better. It''s a great concept and setup.', '2011-08-03 23:44:44.000000', NULL, 1),
(6492226, 964257, 43463, '<td valign="top">A very tasty looking dish and some nice ambience with the candle and wine that invites the viewer to take a seat and dine. One thing confuses me - I see the yummy mushrooms, the thin wafers of parmigian/reggiano cheese, and the colorful greens of the basil but where are the reds of the tomatoes? If it''s the sauce then that is what it is, but all the other ingredients mentioned in the title are very visible which is what makes me think I *should* be seeing tomatoes in the dish. Two critiques on this composition - the reflection of the dish on the wine glass is a bit distracting - a good polarizer lens on the camera would eliminate that reflection. Perhaps a small spotlight shone on the wineglass could have also cut the reflection and illuminated that deep red wine in the glass to make the image even more appealing.</td>', 'A very tasty looking dish and some nice ambience with the candle and wine that invites the viewer to take a seat and dine. One thing confuses me - I see the yummy mushrooms, the thin wafers of parmigian/reggiano cheese, and the colorful greens of the basil but where are the reds of the tomatoes? If it''s the sauce then that is what it is, but all the other ingredients mentioned in the title are very visible which is what makes me think I *should* be seeing tomatoes in the dish. Two critiques on this composition - the reflection of the dish on the wine glass is a bit distracting - a good polarizer lens on the camera would eliminate that reflection. Perhaps a small spotlight shone on the wineglass could have also cut the reflection and illuminated that deep red wine in the glass to make the image even more appealing.', '2011-08-04 01:05:05.000000', NULL, 1),
(6492244, 964277, 43463, '<td valign="top">Looks like a nice arrangement but I think the composition would be more stronger if you pulled back a bit more to show off the sashimi. Sounds odd but I am having trouble finding where my eye should settle and focus on. The blur of the green in the forefront center and the orange tones of the fish right behind it (some are in focus but the ones to the front are not) draw attention away from where the camera seemed to focus on the center of the dish. Pulling back a few inches would help the viewer appreciate the dish as a whole rather than the real close up view. <i>b]OR[/b]</i> you could increase the aperature (14 or higher) and adjust the shutter speed accordingly to a slower one to allow correct exposure to achieve a greater depth of field so that foreground and background are in nice focus. But it also means that with the slower shutter speed you will need to use a tripod to avoid blur - but the positive is that you will get a nice crisp shot of the full image. My taste buds have just now been getting into some sushi''s but have not tried sashimi...that may have to be next on my list:-)</td>', 'Looks like a nice arrangement but I think the composition would be more stronger if you pulled back a bit more to show off the sashimi. Sounds odd but I am having trouble finding where my eye should settle and focus on. The blur of the green in the forefront center and the orange tones of the fish right behind it (some are in focus but the ones to the front are not) draw attention away from where the camera seemed to focus on the center of the dish. Pulling back a few inches would help the viewer appreciate the dish as a whole rather than the real close up view. b]OR[/b] you could increase the aperature (14 or higher) and adjust the shutter speed accordingly to a slower one to allow correct exposure to achieve a greater depth of field so that foreground and background are in nice focus. But it also means that with the slower shutter speed you will need to use a tripod to avoid blur - but the positive is that you will get a nice crisp shot of the full image. My taste buds have just now been getting into some sushi''s but have not tried sashimi...that may have to be next on my list:-)', '2011-08-04 01:19:42.000000', NULL, 1),
(6492250, 964141, 97225, '<td valign="top">Looks yum!</td>', 'Looks yum!', '2011-08-04 01:25:00.000000', NULL, 1),
